{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "1: Copilot session read",
  "description": "End-to-end: how opening the extension turns on-disk Copilot telemetry into rows in the Sessions view. Every step embeds the numbered sequence diagram and points at the exact code for that step.",
  "isPrimary": true,
  "nextTour": "2: Session detail & token trend",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Step 1 — Open the extension",
      "file": "src/extension/agent-observability-vscode/src/extension.ts",
      "line": 81,
      "description": "![1: Copilot session read](.tours/diagrams/d-1.svg)\n\n**▶ Step 1 of 9 in the sequence diagram — `Dev → extension.ts: Open the extension`**\n\nOpening the workspace fires the extension's single entry point, `activate()`. Everything downstream — sources, views, live updates — is wired here, and the read-only, privacy-first pipeline starts: nothing is read from disk until a view actually asks for it."
    },
    {
      "title": "Step 2 — Build the SourceRegistry",
      "file": "src/extension/agent-observability-vscode/src/extension.ts",
      "line": 106,
      "description": "![1: Copilot session read](.tours/diagrams/d-2.svg)\n\n**▶ Step 2 of 9 in the sequence diagram — `extension.ts → SourceRegistry: activate() builds registry`**\n\n`activate()` builds a `SourceRegistry` over the telemetry sources — `CopilotSource`, the Claude source, the Copilot (Cloud) source, and the Copilot (Autonomous) source. The registry is the seam the views talk to, so no view ever knows which on-disk database it is really reading from."
    },
    {
      "title": "Step 3 — Register the tree views",
      "file": "src/extension/agent-observability-vscode/src/extension.ts",
      "line": 257,
      "description": "![1: Copilot session read](.tours/diagrams/d-3.svg)\n\n**▶ Step 3 of 9 in the sequence diagram — `extension.ts → Sessions view: register tree providers`**\n\nThe Sessions tree is registered with `createTreeView(..., { canSelectMany: true })` so several sessions can be selected and combined. Registration only wires the provider — still no telemetry is read yet."
    },
    {
      "title": "Step 4 — getChildren() lazy trigger",
      "file": "src/extension/agent-observability-vscode/src/views/sessionsView.ts",
      "line": 34,
      "description": "![1: Copilot session read](.tours/diagrams/d-4.svg)\n\n**▶ Step 4 of 9 in the sequence diagram — `Sessions view → SourceRegistry: getChildren()`**\n\nWhen the user expands the Sessions view, VS Code calls `getChildren`. This is the lazy trigger — the first point where the extension actually goes looking for data."
    },
    {
      "title": "Step 5 — View asks the source for sessions",
      "file": "src/extension/agent-observability-vscode/src/views/sessionsView.ts",
      "line": 85,
      "description": "![1: Copilot session read](.tours/diagrams/d-5.svg)\n\n**▶ Step 5 of 9 in the sequence diagram — `SourceRegistry → CopilotSource: listSessions()`**\n\nDrilling into a repository, `getSessions` calls `source.listSessions(repository, limit)`. The view holds no data of its own; it always pulls from the source on demand."
    },
    {
      "title": "Step 6 — Read-only snapshot",
      "file": "src/core/agent-observability-core/src/telemetry/snapshot.ts",
      "line": 96,
      "description": "![1: Copilot session read](.tours/diagrams/d-6.svg)\n\n**▶ Step 6 of 9 in the sequence diagram — `CopilotSource → TelemetryDatabase: readonly snapshot + query`**\n\nThe extension's default reader uses `createReadonlySnapshot` to copy the on-disk DB and replay its WAL on that private copy. The desktop instead supplies a native read-only transaction through `TelemetryReadBackend`; it shares the query layer without copying the archive. Neither reader mutates the source."
    },
    {
      "title": "Step 7 — Summary query returns rows",
      "file": "src/core/agent-observability-core/src/telemetry/database.ts",
      "line": 604,
      "description": "![1: Copilot session read](.tours/diagrams/d-7.svg)\n\n**▶ Step 7 of 9 in the sequence diagram — `TelemetryDatabase → CopilotSource: session rows`**\n\n`TelemetryDatabase.listSessions` runs the summary query against the snapshot and returns one row per session — counts, durations, token totals — but no raw prompt or completion text."
    },
    {
      "title": "Step 8 — Source returns SessionSummary[]",
      "file": "src/core/agent-observability-core/src/sources/sessionSource.ts",
      "line": 137,
      "description": "![1: Copilot session read](.tours/diagrams/d-8.svg)\n\n**▶ Step 8 of 9 in the sequence diagram — `CopilotSource → Sessions view: SessionRow[]`**\n\n`CopilotSource.listSessions` hands the `SessionSummary[]` back up through the registry to the view, unchanged. The source is a thin adapter over `TelemetryService`."
    },
    {
      "title": "Step 9 — Paint the session tree",
      "file": "src/extension/agent-observability-vscode/src/views/sessionsView.ts",
      "line": 50,
      "description": "![1: Copilot session read](.tours/diagrams/d-9.svg)\n\n**▶ Step 9 of 9 in the sequence diagram — `Sessions view → Dev: render session tree`**\n\nFinally the view turns those summaries into tree items in `getRoots`/`getRepositories`, and the Sessions panel paints. The loop stays lazy: collapse and re-expand and the whole chain runs again."
    }
  ]
}
